{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794345051385,"sessionID":"ses_e38be2834ffeyfE9hooqtrOekt","part":{"id":"prt_1c74ca4e5001ZCH26yqWNNk6vy","messageID":"msg_1c74c7b37001Rg0RIGCJLZa7NS","sessionID":"ses_e38be2834ffeyfE9hooqtrOekt","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794345051385,"sessionID":"ses_e38be2834ffeyfE9hooqtrOekt","part":{"id":"prt_1c74ca4e8001fND30YLyr62yX9","messageID":"msg_1c74c7b37001Rg0RIGCJLZa7NS","sessionID":"ses_e38be2834ffeyfE9hooqtrOekt","type":"text","text":"<think>No browser available. I'll do an SSR smoke test with a stubbed window to catch runtime errors in components, plus verify the built assets load.\n\nActually, a simpler and effective approach: use `vite build --ssr` with a small entry file that renders the routes to HTML strings, with a minimal global stub for `window`/`localStorage`. Let me do that in a temp file inside the frontend (then delete it).\n\nLet me write `frontend/ssr-check.tsx` (outside src so it isn't part of the app build; but tsconfig includes only \"src\" — I'll put it in a temp dir and build with vite ssr).\n\nPlan:\n```tsx\n// frontend/ssr-check.tsx\nimport { renderToString } from \"react-dom/server\";\nimport { StaticRouter } from \"react-router-dom/server\";\nimport App from \"./src/App\";\n\nconst storage = new Map<string, string>();\n(globalThis as any).window = {\n  localStorage: {\n    getItem: (k: string) => storage.get(k) ?? null,\n    setItem: (k: string, v: string) => void storage.set(k, v),\n    removeItem: (k: string) => void storage.delete(k),\n  },\n  setTimeout: () => 0,\n  clearTimeout: () => 0,\n};\n(globalThis as any).localStorage = (globalThis as any).window.localStorage;\n\nconst html = renderToString(\n  <StaticRouter location={process.env.PATH ?? \"/signin\"}>\n    <App />\n  </StaticRouter>\n);\nconsole.log(html.slice(0, 2000));\n```\n\nThen `npx vite build --ssr ssr-check.tsx --outDir /tmp/...` — but /tmp writes are blocked by the tool policy? The bash tool blocked `touch /tmp/opencode/probe.txt`. Interesting: /tmp/opencode/* was supposed to be allowed but it was denied. So avoid /tmp writes; use a local dir like `frontend/.ssr-out` and delete it after.\n\nLet me do that.\n\nNote `renderToString` with StaticRouter: the App's AuthProvider's useEffect won't run in SSR, so `ready` stays false → Protected renders the Spinner; SignInPage renders the sign-in form. Good enough to catch import/render errors.\n\nLet me also test rendering the customers page? It requires auth state; in SSR the Protected component shows a spinner. Fine — the main goal is catching syntax/runtime errors in the page components. To smoke-test the pages themselves, I could render them directly (not via App) with mocked data... that's more work. The build already type-checks; runtime errors in pages would mostly be in event handlers (not caught by SSR).\n\nA more thorough approach: install jsdom and write a small test that mounts the app with fetch mocked. That's a decent investment for confidence. jsdom is a dev dependency (~a few MB). Let me consider: the app's main flows are (1) sign in, (2) list, (3) detail, (4) form. A jsdom test with a mocked fetch could exercise these.\n\nGiven \"the sandbox can install packages from the public package registries\", installing jsdom + vitest would be reasonable... but it adds significant dev dependencies to the project's lock file. I could install them in a separate directory (not the project) to avoid polluting the frontend's package.json. But the npm cache is read-only; installing in a scratch dir inside the workspace is fine.\n\nLet me weigh: the value is verifying the frontend actually works (catching runtime errors). I think it's worth doing a lightweight version: install jsdom in a scratch dir (e.g. /workspace/.tmp-test) with its own package.json, and run a script that uses the built app? The built app is a browser bundle; I can load it in jsdom with a script tag and a mocked fetch. That's actually a neat approach: build the frontend (done), create an HTML file, use jsdom with `runScripts: \"dangerously\"` and a resource loader that maps /assets/* to the dist files, and a fetch mock backed by a small in-memory API. Then assert the DOM shows the sign-in form, sign in, and see the customer table.\n\nThat's a decent end-to-end frontend test. jsdom's fetch support: jsdom doesn't implement fetch, but I can inject `window.fetch = mock` before scripts run (using `beforeParse`). Also need `window.matchMedia`? Not used. React 19 needs `MessageChannel`? React's scheduler uses MessageChannel — jsdom provides MessageChannel. OK.\n\nLet me try it. Steps:\n1. mkdir /workspace/.e2e (outside frontend) — hmm, it will be in the repo. I'll delete it afterward, or keep it? Better to delete after use to keep the deliverable clean. Actually, I could put the test in `frontend/tests/` and add jsdom as a devDependency of the frontend project. That would make the project have a real frontend test. But it also adds install weight to the Docker build (dev deps are installed in the frontend stage). jsdom is ~10MB, fine but unnecessary for the final image (the frontend stage is separate from the final image; only dist is copied). So dev deps don't bloat the final image.\n\nI'll do the scratch approach: create /workspace/.e2e with its own package.json (jsdom), run the test, then delete the directory. This keeps the deliverable clean.\n\nLet me first do the quick SSR smoke test to catch obvious errors, then decide on the jsdom test.\n\nActually, let me go straight to the jsdom E2E test — it covers rendering of all pages. If jsdom proves painful, I'll fall back to SSR.\n\nLet me set it up.</think>\n\nLet me verify the frontend actually works by driving the built bundle in jsdom with a mocked API.","time":{"start":1794345051368,"end":1794345051381}}}
