{"type":"text","timestamp":1794345106341,"sessionID":"ses_e38be2834ffeyfE9hooqtrOekt","part":{"id":"prt_1c74d7b8f001kOF9ubZN64h5JD","messageID":"msg_1c74d6c5c001TYan04n6JwZfkp","sessionID":"ses_e38be2834ffeyfE9hooqtrOekt","type":"text","text":"<think>The customer list failed to load: \"The customer list could not be loaded. Please try again.\" — an error in my mock fetch. Let me debug: the fetch mock is called with `init.headers` being a `Headers` object (since api.ts creates `new Headers(init.headers)` and passes it). My mock does `init.headers?.Authorization` — for a Headers object, that property doesn't exist (Headers uses .get()). So `token` is empty → returns 401 → the list fails.\n\nLet me fix the mock to read headers properly:\n```js\nconst headers = init.headers instanceof window.Headers ? Object.fromEntries(init.headers.entries()) : (init.headers ?? {});\nconst auth = headers.Authorization ?? headers.authorization ?? \"\";\n```\n\nLet me update the test.\n\nAlso note the app's list error message displayed properly, which shows the error path works.\n\nLet me fix and rerun.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794345106319,"end":1794345106336}}}
