{"type":"text","timestamp":1794345358626,"sessionID":"ses_e38be2834ffeyfE9hooqtrOekt","part":{"id":"prt_1c751550b001zhk5a9TbRx6WZS","messageID":"msg_1c751477a001LvlEQF6kuDM7yX","sessionID":"ses_e38be2834ffeyfE9hooqtrOekt","type":"text","text":"<think>The command timed out again — the `pkill -f gunicorn` inside the command may have killed the shell's process group? Hmm, `pkill -f gunicorn` matches the pattern \"gunicorn\" in the command line — including possibly the bash command itself (which contains \"gunicorn\" in its command line!). That would kill the shell running the command → the tool waits until timeout.\n\nYes: the bash -c command line contains \"gunicorn\", so pkill kills itself. I need a pattern that doesn't match the shell command, e.g. `pkill -f \"[g]unicorn\"`.\n\nLet me check the state and use a safer pattern.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794345358603,"end":1794345358622}}}
